Context: The Pod Count Summary widget on the central dashboard shows running/not-running pod counts per application. It previously read from a cached job-history API and displayed data that could be several hours stale. We changed it so manual and auto refresh always trigger the live pod-status job, with that same run also feeding each application's own status console page, so clicking through from the widget doesn't land on blank/stale data. That change introduced new problems that need fixing.

Current problems:
1. On refresh, the pod-status job runs one application at a time (sequentially) instead of in parallel.
2. The widget's data is not persisted. Clicking refresh again, or navigating to another tab/page and back before the next refresh, causes the data to revert to blank/dash instead of showing the last successfully fetched values.
3. There is no visual indication while a refresh is in progress.
4. If the job fails for one application, it currently affects the rest of the widget instead of only that one application's cell.
5. The "Last updated" timestamp above the widget needs to always accurately reflect when the data was actually fetched, not just when the UI last rendered.

Required changes:

1. Parallel execution: trigger the pod-status job for all applications concurrently on both manual and auto refresh, not sequentially. Don't kick off a new job for an application whose previous job is still running.

2. Persistence: store the last successfully fetched pod-status data per application, with its own fetch timestamp, in state that survives tab switches, navigating away and back, and ideally a page reload (a short-lived server-side cache in addition to frontend state would help here). Never clear a cell's displayed data when a refresh starts — only overwrite it once fresh data actually arrives for that application.

3. Auto-refresh interval: change it from 5 minutes to 10 minutes for this widget, since it now triggers real jobs instead of reading a cache.

4. Per-application loading state: while an application's job is in flight, show a small non-blocking spinner on that row, layered over or beside the existing last-known values — never an empty cell.

5. Progressive, independent updates: update each application's row as soon as its own job completes. Do not wait for every application to finish before updating any of them. One application's success or failure must not block or delay another's.

6. Failure isolation: if a job fails for an application, show a clear failure indicator on that row (e.g. a warning icon or "Load failed" label) instead of a blank or a dash, keeping the last known good data visible underneath if there is any. That indicator should let the user get to that application's job console/logs to investigate. Other applications must keep updating normally, unaffected.

7. Timestamp accuracy: the "Last updated" label above the widget must reflect the true fetch time, not an independent UI timestamp.
   a. Show the top-level "Last updated" as the time the full refresh batch settled (every application's job either succeeded or failed for that cycle).
   b. Also show a per-application timestamp on each row reflecting exactly when that application's data was last successfully fetched, since applications finish at different times within the same cycle.
   While a refresh is in progress, the top-level label should say so explicitly (e.g. "Refreshing..." or "Updating X of Y") instead of silently showing the old timestamp as current.

8. Consistency between widget and status console: an application's status console page must use the exact same data and timestamp as its row in the widget — never two independent fetches that can drift apart.

Design decision for how partial completion is shown: update progressively per application as each job finishes (point 5), rather than waiting for the slowest job before showing anything. Waiting for all of them means one hung job blocks the whole widget and users can't tell which application is actually the straggler; per-row spinners and per-row failure indicators give an accurate real-time picture instead.

Please implement the above, and flag anything in the existing state management, caching layer, or job-trigger mechanism that needs to change to support this cleanly.
